# Courses (N5–N1 + Frontend)

All course levels share ONE engine. Adding/extending a level = editing data files; the pages are generic and take a `level: CourseLevel` prop. Levels live in `COURSE_LEVELS = ['n5','n4','n3','n2','n1','frontend']`.

## Lesson count is FLEXIBLE (IMPORTANT)
- Do NOT cap the number of lessons per level. Author as many lessons as needed to cover the FULL standard JLPT grammar syllabus for that level (N5–N1). The `frontend` track covers practical frontend/BRSE Japanese.
- Lesson order may be (re)arranged freely. Compose part files and wrap with `withSequentialNumbers(...)` (in `types.ts`) so `id` becomes `lesson-<n>` and `number` becomes `1..N` automatically — no manual renumbering.
- Everything is data-driven: the `lessons` array length drives routes, nav, sitemap, and the quiz/reading builders — so the count can grow freely with no code changes.

## Content completeness (IMPORTANT)
- NEVER include fewer items than the source syllabus/textbook. For each lesson, the `vocab` list, the `phrases` (会話 set expressions), AND the `grammar` points must ALL cover the FULL set from the corresponding textbook lesson (N5 follows Minna no Nihongo Shokyu I). Do not trim, sample, or make a "short version".
  - Vocab: include every 単語 word (core words, suffixes like `〜さん/〜じん/〜さい`, plain + polite variants such as `なんさい`/`おいくつ`).
  - Phrases: include every 会話 set expression (e.g. `はじめまして`, `どうぞよろしくおねがいします`, `しつれいですが`, `こちらは〜さんです`).
  - Grammar: include every 文型/sentence pattern taught in that lesson (e.g. L2 must have `これ/それ/あれ`, `この/その/あの＋N`, `N の N`, `N は なんですか`, `はい そうです / いいえ ちがいます`).
  - Pair questions with answers: whenever a grammar point teaches how to ASK something (a か yes/no question, or a question word like 何/どこ/だれ/いくら/なんじ), ALWAYS show how to ANSWER it. Do NOT make the answers a separate grammar point; attach them to the SAME `GrammarPoint` via its optional `answers` block (`{ explanation?, examples }`), which `GrammarPointCard` renders as a separate labelled "Answers"/"Cách trả lời" sub-block inside the point. E.g. the か question point carries `はい、そうです / はい、～です / いいえ、ちがいます` in `answers`, and `N は なんですか` carries `(これ/それ)は N です` in `answers`.
- Only the word/phrase/pattern LISTS must match the textbook. Example sentences, 会話 dialogues, and reading passages must be ORIGINAL — never copy the textbook's copyrighted text.
- When adding/reviewing a lesson, cross-check the textbook's full vocab + expression + grammar lists before finishing; a missing item is a bug.
- Ruby in authored sentences: every kanji in a `GrammarExample`, `ConversationLine`, or `ReadingLine` `jp` string needs a `ruby` segment in surface order - including question words (何, 何時), verbs (行, 食), and holiday names. Partial ruby (e.g. only 行 in 何で 行きますか) is a bug.
- Every `VocabItem` (and reference `kind: 'vocab'` item) with a `kanji` form needs authored `ruby` - one segment per kanji, not a compound `{ base:'教室', ... }` - for `<VocabHeadword>` and the kanji-script vocab quiz.
- When a word has a standard kanji form taught in the lesson, write it as kanji in `jp` (with `ruby`) - not kana-only (e.g. 何ですか not なんですか, 本 not ほん). Keep title, explanation, and examples consistent. Grammar `pattern` strings follow the same rule (e.g. `N は 何 ですか` not `N は なん ですか`); add `patternRuby` when the pattern chip shows kanji.
- Grammar `explanation` / `answers.explanation` strings that cite Japanese (e.g. 何時, 3月25日に) need matching `explanationRuby` on the point or answers block (same segment order in en + vi).
- Grammar `title` copy that embeds kanji (e.g. `Hỏi "là cái gì?" với 何`) needs `titleRuby` on the point.
- Reference list rows with a Japanese `number` date (e.g. 5月3日, 7月第3月曜日) need matching `numberRuby` for every kanji in that string, same as `ruby` on `jp`. A list `introTerm` (e.g. 祝祭日) needs `introTerm.ruby` too.

## Reference section (IMPORTANT)
- `reference?: ReferenceBlock[]` is NOT limited to vocabulary. Each block has a `kind`:
  - `vocab` - titled word grid (included in vocabulary quiz + kanji writing pools via `referenceVocabItems()` when `includeInQuiz` is not false; quiz uses core vocab by default with an optional toggle).
  - `steps` - numbered how-to with optional `notes` footnotes (e.g. public phone use).
  - `list` - labelled rows (e.g. emergency numbers: `number`, optional `jp` + `ruby`, `meaning`; optional `note` on its own row). Use `layout: 'stacked'` for floor guides; optional `numberMeaning` + `numberRuby` translate the row label.
  - `address` - sample multi-line `jp` address + `parts[]` with bilingual labels.
  - `table` - three-column rows (e.g. country · nationality · language) with optional ruby and `speech` for TTS.
- Author as many blocks as the textbook reference section needs; render via `<ReferenceBlockView>` on the lesson page.

## Reading passages (IMPORTANT)
- Each lesson may have **zero or more** `reading: ReadingPassage[]` entries - no fixed count per lesson or per passage.
- Author as many passages (and as many comprehension questions per passage) as needed so reading practice covers **most of the lesson's vocab themes, grammar patterns, and conversation topics**. Split by theme when one passage would cram too much (e.g. shop hours vs daily schedule vs telling the time).
- Passage text must be **original** (not copied from the textbook) and use only vocabulary/grammar from that lesson (or earlier levels when natural). Optional `ruby` on `ReadingLine` where kanji appear.
- `reading-page.tsx` renders **every** passage on the page and quizzes through **all** questions across all passages in one session.

## Data (`src/constants/courses/`)
- One FOLDER per level/track: `n5/`, `n4/`, `n3/`, `n2/`, `n1/`, `frontend/`. Each folder has an `index.ts` that exports its `Course` (`n5Course`, `n4Course`, …, `frontendCourse`) and keeps long content split into `lessons-*.ts` part files inside the same folder (e.g. `n2/lessons-1.ts`, `frontend/lessons-a.ts`). The `courses/` root holds registry/types/levels only — no per-level content.
- Course domain TYPES (`Bilingual`, `VocabItem`, `GrammarExample`, `GrammarPoint`, `ReadingLine`, `ReadingChoice`, `ReadingQuestion`, `ReadingPassage`, `Lesson`, `Course`, `CourseLevel`, ...) are declared in `@/types/course.ts`. `constants/courses/types.ts` re-exports them (`export * from '@/types/course.ts'`) and keeps the RUNTIME `withSequentialNumbers(lessons)` helper. Content is bilingual (`{ en, vi }`), NOT translation keys.
- `levels.ts` – `COURSE_LEVELS`, `isCourseLevel`, and path helpers (`coursePath`, `lessonPath`, `lessonVocabularyPath`, `lessonGrammarPath`, `lessonReadingPath`, `lessonWritingPath`).
- `index.ts` – registry + helpers: `courses`, `courseList`, `getCourse`, `getLesson`, `lessonHasReading`, `COURSE_SITEMAP_PATHS` (appended to `SITEMAP_PATHS` in `routes.ts`); re-exports `types.ts`, `levels.ts`, `summaries.ts`, `seo.ts`. Each `Course` has `level`, `code`, `name`/`subtitle`/`intro`/`seoTitle`/`seoDescription` (all `Bilingual`) and a `lessons` array. Each lesson has `vocab`, `grammar`, and optional `reading` (`ReadingPassage[]` with `lines` + multiple-choice `questions`).

## Quizzes: NO romaji (IMPORTANT)
- Do NOT show romaji/phonetic transcription in the quiz surfaces (vocabulary / grammar / reading). Quiz prompts and reading passages display Japanese (and meanings) only — no romaji helper line. Romaji stays on the lesson detail page only.

## Pages (`src/features/course/`) — generic, take a `level: CourseLevel` prop
- `course-page.tsx` – course overview (lesson list, from course data).
- `lesson-page.tsx` – lesson detail (vocab + grammar with `SpeakButton`s) and CTAs to practice / reading (reading button only if `lessonHasReading`) / writing.
- `exercise-page.tsx` + `course-quiz.ts` – mixed quiz (`ChoiceQuestion` + particle-cloze `InputQuestion`); the builder takes the whole `Course` for distractor pools.
- `reading-page.tsx` – reading comprehension: shows **all** passages (per-line `SpeakButton`, show/hide translation toggle), then steps through multiple-choice questions from every passage; reuses `ResultScreen`.
- `shared.tsx` – `LessonNotFound`, `ResultScreen` (both take `level`).
- Quiz state resets by remounting via `key` (e.g. `key={`${level}:${lesson.id}:${locale}`}`), NOT via `setState` in an effect.

## Wiring
- Routes are file-based under `app/[locale]/[jlptLevel]/**/page.tsx`: `/:level`, `/:level/:lessonId`, `.../vocabulary`, `.../grammar`, `.../reading`, `.../writing` (no `/lesson/` segment). Each route file awaits `[jlptLevel]` and passes it as a prop; the view reads `lessonId` via `useParams`. `generateStaticParams` comes from `@/i18n/route-helpers` (`courseLevelParams`, `courseLessonParams`, and grammar/reading/writing-filtered variants). `routes.ts` declares per-level index entries for SEO keys.
- `getSeoRouteKey` maps any `/n5`…`/n1` (and `/frontend`) path to that level key; `src/i18n/seo-meta.ts` builds SEO from the course's `seoTitle`/`seoDescription` (via `isCourseLevel` + `getCourse`), consumed by `generateMetadata` in each route file.
- Each course is a nav group generated from the registry in `src/constants/nav-items.ts`; `app-drawer-content.tsx` renders course-group labels via `group.label[locale]`, while the Home/Alphabet groups use `labelKey` + `t()`.
- Shared course UI strings live under the `course.*` key namespace in `translations.ts` (en + vi). Per-level titles/intros come from course DATA, not translations.
